---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="What is expected of contributors to MathemAct, what is not tolerated, and what happens when the line is crossed."
  lead="Scepticism is the point of this site. Contempt is not. This page is the difference between them."
  updated="August 2026"
>
  <p class="status">
    This is the short version, and it is the one that matters day to day. The full text —
    adapted from the Contributor Covenant, with the enforcement ladder written out — is
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/blob/main/docs/code-of-conduct.md`}>in the repository</a>.
    Where the two differ, the full text governs and this page is what needs fixing.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>

  <p>
    This applies to everything posted here — reports, comments, ratings, flags — and to
    messages sent to the addresses on this site. It applies to everyone equally, including
    whoever is doing the moderating.
  </p>

  <h2>What is expected</h2>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Argue with the account, not the person</dt>
    <dd>
      <q>This verification step does not rule out the error I would worry about</q> is the
      kind of comment this site is for.
      <q>Anyone who trusts a chatbot with a proof is not a serious mathematician</q> is not.
      The first can be answered; the second only makes people stop posting.
    </dd>

    <dt>Assume the account is honest until you have reason not to</dt>
    <dd>
      People are describing their own work, often work that did not go well. Doubt a
      claim, ask what was checked, ask for the transcript — but start from the assumption
      that the person is flagging in good faith.
    </dd>

    <dt>Respect pseudonyms absolutely</dt>
    <dd>
      Admitting to heavy AI use still carries professional risk, and some of the most
      valuable accounts here will only ever be posted under a pseudonym. Do not try to
      work out who someone is, do not speculate publicly about it, and do not hint that
      you have guessed. This is the rule most likely to get an account removed without a
      warning first.
    </dd>

    <dt>Do not weaponise a disclosure</dt>
    <dd>
      Someone who posts here has told you something about their report that they were
      under no obligation to tell anyone. Using it against them — in a referee report, a
      hiring discussion, a grant panel, or online — defeats the entire purpose of the
      project.
    </dd>

    <dt>Post other people's material only if you may</dt>
    <dd>
      Transcript excerpts must have third-party unpublished material removed before you
      paste them. Unpublished proofs, referee reports, and private correspondence are not
      yours to reproduce, whoever typed them into the model.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What is not acceptable</h2>

  <ul class="tombstone-list">
    <li>Harassment, intimidation, or sustained hostility toward a person.</li>
    <li>
      Attempting to identify a pseudonymous contributor, or publishing anyone's personal
      information.
    </li>
    <li>
      Demeaning remarks about someone's nationality, gender, religion, disability, age,
      institution, or career stage.
    </li>
    <li>
      Posting an account of work you did not do, or fabricating a transcript, an outcome,
      or a verification step.
    </li>
    <li>Promotion of a product, a service, or yourself in place of a genuine account.</li>
    <li>Reproducing unpublished material belonging to someone who has not agreed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Disagreement is not on that list, and will not be added to it. Reporting that a widely
    praised tool failed you, or that a widely condemned one worked, is a contribution and
    is treated as one.
  </p>

  <h2>Flagging</h2>

  <p>
    Every report and comment can be flagged from the page it appears on. If it concerns
    a moderator, or you would rather it did not go through the site, write to
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tell us what you saw and where. You do not have to explain why it upset you, and you
    will not be asked to justify flagging something.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens next</h2>

  <p>
    Moderation here is volunteer-run. That means it is careful rather than fast, and it is
    honest to say so up front: a flag may take days. Content that looks like it could do
    real harm is hidden while it is looked at, and hiding something is not a finding
    against anyone.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every decision is recorded — who took it, when, and why — in a log the moderators keep
    on each other. Hiding something preserves it: the text and the name stay, its author can
    see that it was hidden, and it can be put back.
  </p>

  <p>Depending on what happened, the response is one of:</p>

  <ul class="tombstone-list">
    <li>Nothing, with a note to whoever flagged it explaining why.</li>
    <li>A private word with the person who posted.</li>
    <li>The content hidden, with the thread structure left intact.</li>
    <li>The account suspended, or removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If your content is hidden or your account is suspended, you will be told what rule was
    applied and why. You can reply, and a decision will be reconsidered if you have reason
    to think it was wrong. Deanonymisation and harassment do not get a warning first.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>

  <p>
    This document will change as the site meets situations nobody anticipated. Changes are
    dated at the top and their history is in the
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>. The
    <a href={path('/privacy/')}>privacy notice</a> covers what is stored about you, including
    what happens to your content if you leave.
  </p>
</Page>

